"sanskritLocator":"3049006","sanskritFile":"Ram03.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"3eedbf8c4c598e646530fed56ca7e68e39d021f6a65b0a9850bfcd8412d6ae4a","compact":"c6599f2ff3ee765e308322ab5c101dc4cb08c186a9891d5f64ef1276d17f8a54","nasal":"c6599f2ff3ee765e308322ab5c101dc4cb08c186a9891d5f64ef1276d17f8a54"}},{"id":"VRCE-3-49-7","kanda":3,"kandaId":"aranya","sarga":49,"verse":7,"english":"Jatayu, the king of birds, began to withstand in conflict the arrows and weapons darted by Rāvana, and wounded Rāvana's person with his feet supplies with sharpened talons.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-3-51-5"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"3049007","sanskritFile":"Ram03.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"07a9be096eeb44d8d3db0e07c6a84f8be6483b4e01d943fc6627b86c77f91151","compact":"6755de81e7a28a473e27f323ec3cd5d03c30238208efaa7d9bd4727a02352fba","nasal":"6755de81e7a28a473e27f323ec3cd5d03c30238208efaa7d9bd4727a02352fba"}},{"id":"VRCE-3-49-8","kanda":3,"kandaId":"aranya","sarga":49,"verse":8,"english":"Thereupon, to destroy his enemy, that mighty hero, the Ten-necked Ravana, being exercised with ire, took up ten terrible arrows like to the sceptre of Death, and stretching the bow to the full he shot those straight-coursing sharpened shafts at Jatayu, the king of birds.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.696,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-3-51-6"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"3049008","sanskritFile":"Ram03.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"d8cdc911bb0b7096f28b7bf51ed88fc4f3171ecdd01efd53c50c60ac6b44f44d","compact":"69a8297708c21390a65e7d9fe5b7b7f7fb48d3e993927b1920abdb6a76b1dedf","nasal":"66440e06ae7cc1817e0376157868c1c66c8b3d630d4e03ae3968be02aa5abf81"}},{"id":"VRCE-3-49-9","kanda":3,"kandaId":"aranya","sarga":49,"verse":9,"english":"Thereupon, to destroy his enemy, that mighty hero, the Ten-necked Ravana, being exercised with ire, took up ten terrible arrows like to the sceptre of Death, and stretching the bow to the full he shot those straight-coursing sharpened shafts at Jatayu, the king of birds.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.816,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-3-51-6"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"3049009","sanskritFile":"Ram03.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"68a37d2ffa04e1e401ea4e703aedca6efecc61901c79e9a9598381004d0f2739","compact":"12feb7f10aad0f1e8cfe06166617dae9d52a8c3f200b50d92cee0edf051e06aa","nasal":"12feb7f10aad0f1e8cfe06166617dae9d52a8c3f200b50d92cee0edf051e06aa"}},{"id":"VRCE-3-49-10","kanda":3,"kandaId":"aranya","sarga":49,"verse":10,"english":"Beholding Jānaki with tears in her eyes in that Raksasa's car, Jatayu, the king of birds, disregarding those arrows, darted towards Rāvana, and with his feet broke asunder his bow with the arrows, adorned with pearls and diamonds.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.881,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-3-51-7"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"3049010","sanskritFile":"Ram03.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"51d47ccc0255fb8143f3692e2d27f9afb17552782b4da9d81b685edac594a8dc","compact":"de7fdb6aa36cf9c5952527c2fd399e8b2452a16c6af43ce0671ae212e3d5a13f","nasal":"1e2bd65db0ef21b0f5f787bce95e7921c423233ee56a6b83dc555c1b2b686dca"}},{"id":"VRCE-3-49-11","kanda":3,"kandaId":"aranya","sarga":49,"verse":11,"english":"Beholding Jānaki with tears in her eyes in that Raksasa's car, Jatayu, the king of birds, disregarding those arrows, darted towards Rāvana, and with his feet broke asunder his bow with the arrows, adorned with pearls and diamonds.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.891,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-3-51-7"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"3049011","sanskritFile":"Ram03.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"3ddc769c91c446bcbce0f4bc673f40c27657bad0e57b42c5bf71697e106f3446","compact":"138a8ee7ac7a1c70b824d4efc53932ec5d3d7591dee84b8adb30b3ce90ac6018","nasal":"c8439da397c2631d78cb465eca889efc86481d0407881d2339e7243d407a584b"}},{"id":"VRCE-3-49-12","kanda":3,"kandaId":"aranya","sarga":49,"verse":12,"english":"Pushing away these arrows by the wind of his wings, he again snapped that mighty bow with his feet, and with a stroke of his wings shattered Rāvaņa's flaming shield like to burning fire.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-3-51-10"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"3049012","sanskritFile":"Ram03.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"f3391c444366058c3622cdcb3d00894eb773165d6e46c7c8a586b83c3ed2a5db","compact":"8727d6f11eeed35f895d7fe0b4beaed7b4d46e98695be6c3a6507be2cffe39c3","nasal":"5424900d26715d67bdb47f6c63b5aecc1f76e8e6966860279704a94b729e0f31"}},{"id":"VRCE-3-49-13","kanda":3,"kandaId":"aranya","sarga":49,"verse":13,"english":"Thereupon moving away with the wind of his wings Rāvana's blazing cuirass resembling flaming fire, Jațāyu in that conflict made away with his fleet-coursing asses having the faces of demons.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.875,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-3-51-11"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"3049013","sanskritFile":"Ram03.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"745c3e0f26b2ac64131eebc66b0f11c44bfecb391bd191e0c9cfa2290c4bdbfc","compact":"e3cc4dfb0aa7cdc7e82b36567c106c4d862aa1966013000f2b3532c3e4a98f58","nasal":"a453625c9d941198bf3bfd9d602518223ddf92df4882919ab6f5da3a3c85c68e"}},{"id":"VRCE-3-49-14","kanda":3,"kandaId":"aranya","sarga":49,"verse":14,"english":"Next crumbling into pieces with his impetus the mighty chariot of Rāvana, coursing at will, flaming like fire having steps studded with jewels, and a wooden pole, and throwing down the umbrella and chowris like to the full moon along with the Rākşasas engaged in carrying them, the effulgent and mighty lord of birds shattered the head of the charioteer with the strokes of his beak.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.755,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-3-51-12"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"3049014","sanskritFile":"Ram03.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"7ac24fa8b92a6d360c51dbf469680ea6e9462761142edb69c904cbd81ef685c7","compact":"9f845f1b57171183b3f1e39b815cbc5a22441d2b3892afae1bdbaf6180eab701","nasal":"692039bd7a3af20fe03fecdcb6625043681fef2e0935f825e0fd7c47a9106175"}},{"id":"VRCE-3-49-15","kanda":3,"kandaId":"aranya","sarga":49,"verse":15,"english":"Having his bow snapped and deprived of his car, horses and charioteer, Rāvana fell down to the earth, taking Vaidehi on his lap.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-3-51-13"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"3049015","sanskritFile":"Ram03.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"426e56f30ad320f738ac928e0b9d92fccaf168a4fe653817a699bfafc52deb7c","compact":"8eb1ea264ac7169a852f2d5d38b1f76c5684ac7a1aeb8c6473b1f1e2b8cb15a3","nasal":"33150ee7564c3ffe0c37de92cdae922100ea2a28170070bb02cd8375850b828d"}},{"id":"VRCE-3-49-16","kanda":3,"kandaId":"aranya","sarga":49,"verse":16,"english":"Beholding Rāvaņa fallen on the ground and of broken conveyance, all creatures praised the king of vultures again and again and worshipped him.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-3-51-14"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"3049016","sanskritFile":"Ram03.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"7b1b03f8353e2648224f073ff3b9643bf13584883bd3526dcde10a73d427ac7c","compact":"a4a3234ddb1cef64768baaec66fb304068ee21c944dbdd79f7b7e0295dfca241","nasal":"a4a3234ddb1cef64768baaec66fb304068ee21c944dbdd79f7b7e0295dfca241"}},{"id":"VRCE-3-49-17","kanda":3,"kandaId":"aranya","sarga":49,"verse":17,"english":"Thereupon, finding the lord of birds worn out on account of his old age, Ravana, highly encouraged, again rose high up in the welkin, taking Maithill with him.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.758,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-3-51-15"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"3049017","sanskritFile":"Ram03.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"d2272745534c8afb13403047bdd721dc3cc29c925433491c3738278ff6366db8","compact":"687ea76896a26a96eb98cea52228ec28c3a26ec7636a517ae4a691bd6d01c07a","nasal":"0514d34868a003b979885d1141e3be61ce14fd126b19ec523e0530f384ef9c15"}},{"id":"VRCE-3-49-18","kanda":3,"kandaId":"aranya","sarga":49,"verse":18,"english":"He had all his weapons broken in the conflict, and had but his dagger left to him. beholding him proceed thus pleased, taking the daughter of Janaka on his lap, all his weapons having been lost, with his sword alone left, the mighty and powerful lord of vultures, Jațāyu, rose up and, darting towards Rāvana, resisted him and said,","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.642,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-3-51-16"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"3049018","sanskritFile":"Ram03.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"05628a5b433f4b0f929b84eccd2ee6b1a4c9ff172de564052b7d90b6c525187d","compact":"eb5a30fef0126b0993fda43b07f7ba483d6d9336e301fb7bfad612ad098a2256","nasal":"647555df658e047e1eefc95bd36d069d71c4af641119d54662a38dfdddf1f309"}},{"id":"VRCE-3-49-19","kanda":3,"kandaId":"aranya","sarga":49,"verse":19,"english":"O Rāvana of feeble sense, it is for the destruction of the whole line of Rākşasas that you carries away this spouse of Răma, having arrows like to thunderbolts.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.871,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-3-51-17"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"3049019","sanskritFile":"Ram03.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"e1ad7439c31e3fda717111cc8db089c7acd147c03dd7c49c8221639665d9b6a9","compact":"5db4a7e01d83e02bba9a43abf5d0d98ef533c6dba709c3deda36b9e657ae1df9","nasal":"5db4a7e01d83e02bba9a43abf5d0d98ef533c6dba709c3deda36b9e657ae1df9"}},{"id":"VRCE-3-49-20","kanda":3,"kandaId":"aranya","sarga":49,"verse":20,"english":"Like one thirsty drinking water, you do address yourself to drinking poison along with your friends, courtiers, four-fold forces, servants and relatives.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.86,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-3-51-18"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"3049020","sanskritFile":"Ram03.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"bcef4e34f35d3b329e204b125c66a227632fbbe438a10a2668bd735bf9d203d1","compact":"0ba2b436127af2d934f65e627c6c2b70e8601fd95dcdb52075a0095a6a50ff0e","nasal":"953e6bd4e45ec14e7b705befd7a10d47a4d7b605e06831bd3c7c8691de97e07b"}},{"id":"VRCE-3-49-21","kanda":3,"kandaId":"aranya","sarga":49,"verse":21,"english":"Foolish persons unaware of the fruit of their actions, meet in a short time with their own destruction, so shall you very soon meet with your own end.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-3-51-19"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"3049021","sanskritFile":"Ram03.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"974139947c67a267b94c0d99f0c5460f6b3c2c580146e5774d9ffd013cfbb3e9","compact":"e275935fde6d68ff75729fba8e3e7ad311eba3bcfcdada152ebbfe6635c2449e","nasal":"5a97760a0f41139a1a2ee092d788339692259f6aeb81bf9127b6afc278d36960"}},{"id":"VRCE-3-49-22","kanda":3,"kandaId":"aranya","sarga":49,"verse":22,"english":"You have been bound up by the noose of Death; and, proceeding whither, shall you save yourself, like to fish eating up baits with hooks for their own destruction?","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.845,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-3-51-20"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"3049022","sanskritFile":"Ram03.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"efe595817b4d824cb8e9ba99db586acd63a9b65d8e774a05bf2ac22e0f9a498d","compact":"82bdf6abf3bb9a45637bcc20bb1ed220fde90d6032f1b343982de5280206c59c","nasal":"82bdf6abf3bb9a45637bcc20bb1ed220fde90d6032f1b343982de5280206c59c"}},{"id":"VRCE-3-49-23","kanda":3,"kandaId":"aranya","sarga":49,"verse":23,"english":"O Rāvana, it is beyond your power to defeat the Kakutsthas. They shall not forgive you for this your encroachment upon their asylum.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.871,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-3-51-21"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"3049023","sanskritFile":"Ram03.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"e1458d94f113b562eee375ea25d82aa52136be93d84745646bacec431e0e8b4c","compact":"491f11246b8f103b16bb43f3a4155a443a759b2bedab0cf8a2dddd0af29accab","nasal":"491f11246b8f103b16bb43f3a4155a443a759b2bedab0cf8a2dddd0af29accab"}},{"id":"VRCE-3-49-24","kanda":3,"kandaId":"aranya","sarga":49,"verse":24,"english":"What have been perpetrated by you coward, is blamed by all, and is the way taken recourse to by thieves and not by heroes.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-3-51-22"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"3049024","sanskritFile":"Ram03.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"3b732ddf